{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>They are Dathan and Aviram, etc.</b> One needs to know what this statement comes to teach us. And if it is to inform us that these are the same ones mentioned in the episode of Korah — that is obvious, for there is no room to err that they were other Dathans and Avirams, other sons of Eliav. Furthermore, in the first place there was no need to mention them at all, and they should have been as though they never were, like the rest of the families that perished and were consumed and were not counted. Further, why did he elaborate on these matters: <b>who incited</b>, etc., <b>when they incited against HaShem</b>?" "It appears that the verse comes here only to say that they were the cause of the entire episode of Korah, for HaShem wishes to publicize the wicked who are the cause of the evil done, and thus it says <b>They are Dathan</b>, etc., <b>who incited</b>, etc. — meaning, they incited others against HaShem and against Moses His servant, and so Rashi explained. From this you learn that they were the ones who caused the assembly that gathered to sin. And it is possible that they also caused Korah himself to sin, and although the verse says ״וַיִּקַּח קֹרַח״ (Numbers 16:1), which appears as though he acted on his own, it is possible that this was because of what preceded him from them — and in any case they incited him. And its saying <b>and the earth opened</b>, etc., after these matters are already written in the portion of Korah — the intention is to say that even the swallowing of Korah and the fire consuming the two hundred and fifty men — they were the cause of the entire event from beginning to end. From this you learn that even though the Jews gathered against Moses and against Aharon, they would have repented had it not been for those two wicked men." "<b>And this</b> is your sign that the man Moses exerted himself to appease only them, for it is written (Numbers 16:12): ״וַיִּשְׁלַח מֹשֶׁה לִקְרֹא לְדָתָן״, etc., and it is written (Numbers 16:25): ״וַיֵּלֶךְ מֹשֶׁה אֶל דָּתָן״, etc. — for he sensed that the matter hung on them. From this point on, the souls of all the dead are held against those two wicked men, and this provides an argument of merit on behalf of Korah and the two hundred and fifty men. And so you will find that the verse states in the portion of Eikev (Deuteronomy 11:6): ״וַאֲשֶׁר עָשָׂה לְדָתָן וְלַאֲבִירָם״, etc. — where it enumerates the event only regarding Dathan and Aviram. And accordingly, we can uphold the reasoning of the one who says (Sanhedrin 108a) that the assembly of Korah has no share in the World to Come — in the manner of Dathan and Aviram, and uphold the reasoning of the one who says concerning them that the verse states ״מוֹרִיד שְׁאוֹל וַיָּעַל״ — He brings down to Sheol and brings up — regarding the rest of the assembly, excluding them."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:14:46.519995931Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}